course: biology
lesson: 276
title: How old is it? Rock layers, rock clocks, amber and the long story of life
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Look at the stripes in a cliff: each stripe is a layer of mud, sand or ash that settled on top of the one before, like books piled on a desk, so the lowest layers are usually the oldest.
explain: To get real numbers, scientists use rock clocks: some rocks, especially volcanic ash, hold atoms that slowly turn into other atoms at a steady speed, and measuring how much has changed tells the age, like reading how far a candle has burnt down.
explain: If a fossil lies between two ash layers, one 67 and one 65 million years old, the animal lived in between.
explain: Squeeze Earth's 4.5 billion years into one day and the story is amazing: for most of the day life is only tiny microbes, dinosaurs arrive at about 10.40 at night, vanish at about 11.40, and people turn up a few seconds before midnight.
fact: Rock layers are laid down one on top of another, so lower layers are usually older, and fossils in deeper layers are usually older than fossils near the top.
fact: Some rocks, especially volcanic ash, contain atoms that change into other atoms at a steady rate; measuring how much has changed dates the rock, and ash layers above and below a fossil give its age.
fact: Carbon dating works only for things that lived up to about 50,000 years ago, such as mammoths and old bones; dinosaurs are far too old for it, so other rock clocks are used.
fact: Amber is tree resin that hardened over millions of years; insects stuck in the sticky resin were sealed in, with tiny hairs and wing veins kept.
fact: DNA breaks down over time, and no dinosaur DNA has ever been found; the oldest DNA read so far is about 2 million years old, from soil in Greenland.
fact: If Earth's 4.5 billion years were one day, dinosaurs would arrive at about 10.40 at night, vanish at about 11.40, and humans would appear a few seconds before midnight.
fact: The oldest fossils are about 3.5 billion years old: stromatolites, mounds built by tiny microbes; living stromatolites still grow at Shark Bay in Australia.
fact: For billions of years, all life on Earth was microscopic.
fact: About 540 million years ago many kinds of animals with eyes, shells and legs appeared fairly quickly in the fossil record; this is called the Cambrian explosion, and strange animals like Anomalocaris and five-eyed Opabinia lived then.
fact: Trilobites were sea creatures with jointed legs and hard shells, distant cousins of crabs and insects; they lived for about 270 million years and had some of the first complex eyes, made of crystal.
fact: Tiktaalik was a 375-million-year-old fish with a neck, lungs and strong leg-like fins; scientists guessed which age of rock it should be in, searched, and found it in the Canadian Arctic.
fact: Sea fossils are found at the top of Mount Everest, because that rock formed under the sea and was pushed up as continents collided; mountains still rise a few millimetres a year.
fact: About 250 million years ago the continents were joined in one land called Pangaea, which is why the same fossils are found in Africa and South America; continents drift a few centimetres a year, about as fast as fingernails grow.
exercise: In a cliff with many rock layers, which layers are usually the oldest? | The lowest ones | The highest ones | The ones in the middle | The thickest ones
exercise: Which kind of rock is best for a rock clock? | Volcanic ash | Wet beach sand | Dark garden soil | White chalk dust
exercise: How does a rock clock tell an age? | Some atoms in the rock change into other atoms at a steady speed | The rock grows a new ring every single year, just like a tree trunk | The rock gets heavier every year | The rock changes colour every spring
exercise: How far back can carbon dating reach? | About 50,000 years | About 66 million years | About 4.5 billion years | About 100 years or so
exercise: What is amber? | Tree resin that hardened over millions of years | Honey that turned to stone | Orange glass made by volcanoes when hot lava cooled | Frozen sea water from long ago
exercise: Has any dinosaur DNA ever been found? | No, DNA breaks down long before 66 million years | Yes, from the blood inside mosquitoes kept in amber | Yes, from every dinosaur bone | Yes, but only from T. rex
exercise: How old is the oldest DNA read so far? | About 2 million years | About 66 million years | About 500 years at most | About 1 billion years or more
exercise: If Earth's history were one day, when would humans appear? | A few seconds before midnight | At breakfast time, early in the day | At midday, just as lunch starts | At teatime, a few hours before bed
exercise: What are the oldest fossils, about 3.5 billion years old? | Stromatolites, mounds built by microbes | Dinosaur eggs | Trilobite shells from the bottom of the sea | Fish bones
exercise: What was the Cambrian explosion? | A time when many kinds of animals appeared fairly quickly | A huge volcano that blew up and killed all the dinosaurs | A star that blew up near Earth | The day the Moon was made
exercise: Which strange animal of the Cambrian had five eyes? | Opabinia | Tiktaalik | The trilobite | The ammonite
exercise: What were trilobites? | Sea creatures with jointed legs and hard shells | Early flying birds | Small dinosaurs that lived in the hot dry deserts | Giant land snails
exercise: What made the eyes of trilobites special? | They were some of the first complex eyes, made of crystal | They could see straight through solid rock to find food | They had no eyes at all | Their eyes were on the ends of their legs
exercise: What was Tiktaalik? | A fish with a neck, lungs and leg-like fins | A flying reptile | The first bird, with teeth and long tail feathers | A kind of trilobite
exercise: How did scientists find Tiktaalik? | They guessed which age of rock to search and went looking | A farmer dug it up by chance while ploughing his field | It washed up on a beach | It was frozen in ice
exercise: Why are there sea fossils on top of Mount Everest? | The rock formed under the sea and was pushed up | Birds carried shells up there | Climbers dropped their lunch | The sea once covered the whole Earth up to the top
exercise: What was Pangaea? | One land made of all the continents joined | A giant dinosaur | An old name for the whole of the Pacific Ocean | A volcano in Africa
exercise: How fast do continents drift? | A few centimetres a year, like fingernails grow | A few metres a day, fast enough to see if you watch | A kilometre a year | They never move at all
test: In Jurassic Park, scientists take dinosaur blood from a mosquito in amber and make new dinosaurs. Why can't this really happen? | DNA breaks down long before 66 million years, and no dinosaur DNA has been found | Mosquitoes did not bite dinosaurs, because dinosaur skin was far too thick for them | Amber is too hard to cut | Dinosaurs had no blood
test: «my uncle says they carbon dated a T. rex, that's how they know it's 66 million» What is right? | No, carbon dating reaches only about 50,000 years; dinosaurs are dated by other rock clocks | Yes, carbon dating works for anything, however old it is, so it works on T. rex bones | Yes, but only for teeth | No, dinosaurs can't be dated at all
test: A fossil lies between a layer of ash 67 million years old below and one 65 million years old above. How old is the fossil? | Between 65 and 67 million years | Exactly 132 million years | Younger than 65 million years | Older than 67 million years
test: «found a shell on a mountain, someone must've dropped it from a picnic» What is more likely? | The rock formed under the sea long ago and was pushed up | A seagull carried it all the way up there from the beach | It grew there on its own | The mountain was a beach last year
test: Count them: a trilobite fossil shows 5 pairs of jointed legs. How many legs is that? | 10 | 5 | 12 | 6
test: «so dinos and cavemen fought, like in the Flintstones» What is right? | No, dinosaurs died out about 66 million years before the first humans | Yes, they lived side by side, and cavemen hunted them with spears and clubs | Yes, cavemen kept them as pets | Only in Africa did they meet
test: What would we see if we found the same fossil plant in Africa and South America? | A sign that the continents were once joined | Proof that seeds swam across the ocean | A sign that the fossils are fake | Proof that Africa is older
test: «life always had animals, like from day one» What is right? | No, for billions of years life was only microscopic | Yes, animals came first, then microbes | Yes, dinosaurs were the first life | No, life began only 1,000 years ago
test: A boy holds a piece of amber with a fly inside. Why is the fly so well kept? | It got stuck in sticky resin that sealed it from air | Flies never rot | The fly was dipped in clear glue by a jeweller to sell it | It was frozen in a glacier
test: Count them: a Cambrian scene has 2 Opabinia, each with 5 eyes, and 1 trilobite with 2 eyes. How many eyes in all? | 12 | 10 | 7 | 15
test: «continents moving?? yeah right, it'd be like an earthquake every day» What is right? | They really move, but only a few centimetres a year, too slowly to feel | They do not move at all; the maps are only drawn a bit differently each year | They move a metre every day | They only move during storms
exam: Identify the principle by which the relative ages of sedimentary rock layers are ordinarily determined. | Layers situated lower in a sequence are generally older than those above them | Layers situated higher in a sequence are invariably older than those beneath them | The thickest layer is always the oldest | The age of a layer is determined by its colour alone
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the recovery of dinosaurian DNA from amber. | No such DNA has been recovered, as DNA degrades over far shorter periods | Complete dinosaurian DNA has been recovered on numerous occasions | Such DNA has been recovered exclusively from mosquitoes | Such DNA is recovered routinely by carbon dating
exam: Ascertain the approximate upper limit of the age which may be determined by carbon dating. | Approximately fifty thousand years | Approximately sixty-six million years | Approximately four and a half billion years | Approximately one hundred and fifty years
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the presence of marine fossils upon the summit of Mount Everest? | The rock was formed beneath the sea and subsequently uplifted by the collision of continents | The fossils were deposited there by migrating birds | The fossils were transported there by mountaineers | The summit has been submerged within the present century
